From cb074cfa1623c7e1c130d217b37b17144f3ae124 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: urza <189804+urza@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 12:40:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Tests: perf.mjs measures how responsive Yap feels, build
 A against build B

The instrument for the performance experiments: each experiment is a worktree with a change, measured against the
worktree without it. One person (the seed's admin) on a server with a real history, through three links.

What it measures. perf-seed.mjs builds the server once and keeps it: 30 people, 10 rooms, 3 direct conversations,
50,000 messages spread over 180 days (the lobby holds 20,000), emoji in about 10%, replies 5%, reactions on 3%, 2%
long multi-line texts, 50 pictures and 20 GIFs. It goes through the real routes (sign-in, the send route, tus, the
hub) in parallel and takes about 1.5 minutes; the database is 50 MB. Every measured server starts from a copy, with
the database on, and answers about 3 s after its start. The journeys (perf-journeys.mjs), timed in the page's own
clock: a cold and a warm open (the six phases of the boot report, the files, the bytes on the wire, long tasks, the
heap), a resume after a frozen background, sends (the own row and the server's confirmation), room changes to the
quietest and the busiest room, three older pages, the emoji and the GIF picker (the first open on a device and the
next one), and a cold open and older pages on a phone with a 4x slower CPU. A and B alternate A, B, B, A per journey
and profile; the report gives medians, min, max and deltas, and runs.json every sample.

Why a proxy and not a CDP throttle: a CDP throttle shapes the page's requests only, never the service worker's
fetches or the WebSocket frames, and the shell caching is the worker's work. The proxy sees every byte, so it also
counts the wire, and it can kill the server side of a socket while the client side stays silent, which the resume
needs. It moved from load.mjs into proxy.mjs; load.mjs imports it and passes as before.

Assumptions that turned out wrong, found with an A/A run (the same build on both sides):
- Page.setWebLifecycleState frozen stops nothing in this headless Chromium (Playwright 1.47). The call returns ok,
  the page's timers tick through the freeze, SignalR's own 30 s timeout fires, and the page reconnects before the
  thaw: the return found the rows already there (0 ms). The resume pauses the page in the debugger instead (no
  script runs, as in a suspended app; the overdue timers fire at the return): far 2,980 ms, three round trips.
  load.mjs still uses the freeze call.
- The proxy kept the time of bytes nobody would receive: the server writes whole responses into its queue at once,
  and a page closed during the worker's precache left seconds of them on the link. The next page on that link
  started 2.1 s late. A closing connection now gives its waiting bytes' time back.
- The person's presence depended on the order of the runs: a page that connects after the server saw the person go
  offline gets the person's own join line in the lobby, set in italics, and the italic font (380 KB) loaded before
  the rows. A page idling on Settings keeps the person online on each server for the whole run.

The defaults keep one A/B run at five repetitions near 20 minutes (19.5 measured): no phone rows and no sends on
far, no resume on near and lan, no phone rows on lan. --full runs every journey (28.5 minutes A/B).

